/* Njia — course catalogue
 *
 * DATA PROVENANCE — two separate fields, on purpose.
 *
 * A single `data_confidence` flag used to cover a whole record. It did not:
 * every verification_note ever written here describes a FEE, while the same
 * record also carries `employment_rate` and `median_salary_kes`. The result
 * was invented outcome statistics rendering under a "Verified" badge, and
 * feeding the employment sort, the payback calculation, and the comparison
 * table's "better" winner. A teenager was being told one course beat another
 * on evidence that did not exist. Hence:
 *
 *   fees_confidence     — 'verified' only when total_fees_kes traces to a
 *                         named, dated source recorded in verification_note.
 *   outcomes_confidence — covers employment_rate and median_salary_kes.
 *
 * `outcomes_confidence` is 'illustrative' on every record, and that is not
 * laziness. Programme-level graduate outcome data is not published in Kenya:
 * government only recently began tracking TVET graduates for three years
 * post-completion, and no equivalent per-course series exists for universities.
 * A course-level "78% employment rate" is therefore not merely unsourced — it
 * is unsourceable today. These figures are kept as coarse planning
 * assumptions, labelled as estimates everywhere they appear, and never
 * presented as measurement. The one real anchor is sector-level and lives in
 * SECTOR_EARNINGS (data/labour-market.js) with its citation.
 *
 * If a tracer study ever publishes real per-course outcomes, set
 * outcomes_confidence: 'verified' and cite it — never before.
 */

